#!/usr/bin/env bash

# A loose brick, hidden from careless eyes (you found it with 'ls -a').
# You INSPECTED before acting -- the mark of a careful crawler.
# Pressing it is SAFE: it only opens the hidden passage out of this room.
# It deletes nothing and touches nothing outside this very room.

cat <<'EOF'

You run your fingers along the wall and feel a LOOSE BRICK,

hidden from any careless torch. You found it by looking for what

others miss -- « ls -a » -- and you INSPECTED it before pressing.

You press it firmly...

  *** GRRRRIND ***  A concealed passage opens in the far wall!

EOF

# Reveal the hidden safe passage (in-room rename only; no rm, nothing outside).
mv .safe_passage safe_passage 2>/dev/null

cat <<'EOF' > scroll_of_knowledge
==================  SCROLL OF SAFE PASSAGE  ==================

You survived the Trap Room. Here is why:

  * « ls -a » revealed what a careless torch could not --
    files and rooms whose names begin with a dot are HIDDEN,
    not gone. The careful always check.

  * You INSPECTED before you acted:
        « file name »      tells you WHAT a thing is.
        « head -n 1 name » peeks inside; a '#!' means a script.
    You never run a script -- or open a chest -- blindly.

  * The obvious, shiny path ( the cursed_chest, the dead_end )
    was BAIT. The true way out was hidden, and safe.

Carry this rule with you for every dungeon to come:

        LOOK before you LEAP.  INSPECT before you ACT.

A new passage has opened. Step through it:  « cd safe_passage »
=============================================================
EOF

echo "A « scroll_of_knowledge » materializes at your feet -- read it: « cat scroll_of_knowledge »"
echo "A new passage has opened: « cd safe_passage »"
